WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.623
Lean explained with

00:00:01.435 --> 00:00:02.319
TypeScript

00:00:02.838 --> 00:00:04.312
Niklas Gruhn explains

00:00:04.123 --> 00:00:05.161
how Lean proofs

00:00:04.972 --> 00:00:05.514
work

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:07.177
propositions as types

00:00:06.989 --> 00:00:07.920
and proofs as

00:00:07.732 --> 00:00:08.463
values

00:00:08.628 --> 00:00:09.795
by proving simple

00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:11.104
propositions directly

00:00:10.916 --> 00:00:12.177
in TypeScript's type

00:00:11.989 --> 00:00:12.625
checker

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.423
The Lean verifier

00:00:14.235 --> 00:00:15.213
is essentially a

00:00:15.025 --> 00:00:15.826
type checker

00:00:15.957 --> 00:00:16.581
to prove a

00:00:16.393 --> 00:00:17.666
proposition you write

00:00:17.478 --> 00:00:18.279
a value of

00:00:18.091 --> 00:00:19.164
the matching type

00:00:19.694 --> 00:00:20.590
The post shows

00:00:20.402 --> 00:00:21.545
that TypeScript's type

00:00:21.357 --> 00:00:22.488
system shares enough

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.373
of the fundamentals

00:00:23.185 --> 00:00:24.328
to express simple

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:24.752
proofs

00:00:24.847 --> 00:00:25.637
and uses that

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:26.545
to build intuition

00:00:26.357 --> 00:00:26.969
for Lean

00:00:27.159 --> 00:00:28.750
arguing that LLMs

00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:29.705
writing code plus

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.439
Lean automating verification

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:31.852
is a good

00:00:31.663 --> 00:00:32.335
match

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:33.876
Full story on

00:00:33.687 --> 00:00:34.583
the dashboard
